===Etymology===
From a merger of {{inh|en|enm|tiding}}, {{m|enm|tidinge}}, from Late {{inh|en|ang|tīdung}}, with {{m+|enm|tidinde}}, {{m|enm|tidende}}, from or influenced by {{der|en|non|tíðindi||news, tidings}},<ref>{{R:Webster 1913|tidings}}</ref> both connected to {{m+|ang|tīdan||to befall; happen}}. Though it is sometimes assumed that the form in ''-ind/-end'' is original and later assimilated to {{m|en|-ing}},<ref>T.F. Hoad, ''Concise Dictionary of English Etymology'', {{ISBN|978-0-19-283098-2}}, s.v. ''tidings''</ref> the cognates {{cog|nl|tijding}} and {{cog|de|Zeitung}} point to inheritance from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*tīdungō}}. {{surf|en|tide|t1=time|-ing}}.

